+# CLI Contract: `tarx`
+
+**Feature**: `001-tar-archive-cli` | **Date**: 2026-11-05 | **Spec**: [../spec.md](../spec.md)
+
+This is the project's only external interface: a command-line program. The contract below is
+normative — the test suite asserts against these exact strings and statuses, and nothing outside
+this document is part of the public surface.
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m tarx list    ARCHIVE
+python -m tarx extract ARCHIVE DEST [--overwrite]
+```
+
+- Requires `src/` on `PYTHONPATH` (the package lives at `src/tarx/`).
+- `prog` is set explicitly to `tarx`, so usage text reads `usage: tarx ...` rather than
+  `usage: __main__.py ...` (research R7).
+- Exactly two subcommands exist; both are required to be spelled in full (FR-001).
+- No environment variables, config files, or stdin input affect behavior.
+
+## Commands
+
+### `list ARCHIVE`
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Positional | `ARCHIVE` — path to a tar or gzip-compressed tar archive (exactly one; FR-002) |
+| Options | none |
+| stdout | one line per member: the member's recorded name followed by `\n` (FR-005) |
+| Order | the archive's recorded order; no sorting, filtering, or de-duplication (FR-006) |
+| Membership | every member, including directories and non-file/non-directory entries (FR-007) |
+| Directory names | no trailing path separator (FR-008) |
+| stderr on success | empty (FR-009) |
+| Exit on success | `0` |
+
+Empty archive → stdout empty, exit `0`.
+
+### `extract ARCHIVE DEST [--overwrite]`
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Positionals | `ARCHIVE`, then `DEST` — exactly two (FR-003) |
+| Options | `--overwrite` — replace existing regular files at colliding paths (FR-021) |
+| stdout | exactly one line: `extracted N files\n` (FR-014) |
+| `N` | decimal count of distinct regular files written; excludes directories and skipped members; the literal word `files` is used for every `N`, including `0` and `1` (FR-015) |
+| stderr on success | empty (FR-014) |
+| Exit on success | `0` |
+
+Write behavior:
+
+- Regular-file members are recreated at `DEST/<member name>` with byte-identical contents (FR-010).
+- Directories needed by a member, and directory members with no files in them, are created (FR-011).
+- `DEST` and any missing parents are created when absent (FR-012).
+- Non-colliding pre-existing entries under `DEST` are left untouched (FR-013).
+- Members that are neither regular files nor directories are skipped without failing (FR-016).
+- Members are written in archive order, so a path stored twice ends with the later entry's contents
+  and is counted once.
+
+## Compression
+
+Both subcommands accept uncompressed and gzip-compressed tar archives and behave identically
+(FR-017). Compression is detected from the archive's **content**, never its name, so `.tar`,
+`.tar.gz`, `.tgz`, and unconventional names all work, including a gzip archive named `.tar`
+(FR-018, research R2).
+
+## Exit statuses
+
+Used consistently and exclusively (FR-028):
+
+| Status | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `0` | Success |
+| `2` | Every failure except an overwrite conflict — unusable archive, unusable destination, kind collision, member path escaping the destination, permission error, invocation error |
+| `3` | `extract` without `--overwrite` would have replaced an existing regular file |
+
+Status `3` is reserved for the one condition that re-running with `--overwrite` resolves. When both
+a status-2 condition and a status-3 condition are present, **status 2 is reported** (research R8).
+
+Validation order within `extract`:
+
+1. Archive is readable and parses → else `2`
+2. `DEST` exists and is not a directory → `2`
+3. Any member path escapes the destination → `2`
+4. Any kind collision → `2`
+5. Any overwrite conflict and no `--overwrite` → `3`
+6. Writes begin
+
+Steps 1–5 touch no files, so a failure in any of them leaves the destination exactly as it was
+(FR-020).
+
+## Diagnostics
+
+Every failure writes a short, human-readable, single-line diagnostic to stderr that names the
+offending path or member, and writes nothing to stdout (FR-027). No failure is signalled by exit
+status alone, and no internal traceback ever reaches the user.
+
+Format: `tarx: <message>` on one line.
+
+`tarfile` raises a multi-line `ReadError` that enumerates every compression method it tried
+(research R4); that text is never forwarded. The messages below are the project's own.
+
+| Condition | Message | Status | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Archive path missing | `cannot read archive '<path>': no such file` | 2 | FR-024 |
+| Archive is a directory | `cannot read archive '<path>': is a directory` | 2 | FR-024 |
+| Archive unreadable | `cannot read archive '<path>': permission denied` | 2 | FR-024 |
+| Not a tar archive | `'<path>' is not a valid tar archive` | 2 | FR-024 |
+| `DEST` exists as non-directory | `destination '<path>' exists and is not a directory` | 2 | FR-025 |
+| `DEST` cannot be created/written | `cannot write to destination '<path>': <reason>` | 2 | FR-025 |
+| Member escapes destination | `refusing to extract member '<name>' outside destination` | 2 | FR-026 |
+| File member vs. existing directory | `cannot replace directory '<path>' with file from member '<name>'` | 2 | FR-023 |
+| Directory member vs. existing non-directory | `cannot replace '<path>' with directory from member '<name>'` | 2 | FR-023 |
+| Overwrite conflict, no `--overwrite` | `'<path>' already exists; use --overwrite to replace it` | 3 | FR-019 |
+
+Paths in diagnostics are shown as given on the command line, joined with the member name where a
+member is involved, so the message is actionable without the user re-deriving it.
+
+Invocation errors (no subcommand, unknown subcommand, wrong argument count, unknown option) produce
+`argparse`'s usage guidance on stderr and exit `2`, with no other action taken (FR-004). This is
+`argparse`'s own default behavior and is therefore not restated in the table above.
+
+## Out of contract
+
+Not provided, and asserted nowhere: archive creation, member selection or filtering, compression
+formats beyond detection-by-content, reading archives from stdin or a URL, preservation of
+permissions / ownership / modification times, recursion into nested archives, progress output,
+colors, headers, or totals beyond the specified lines.
